# Rigor core overlay — supplemental core signatures.
#
# --- OpenStruct#method_missing / #respond_to_missing? (issue #1456) ---
#
# rbs 4.2 ships no `ostruct` signatures (since Ruby 4.0 `ostruct` is a bundled
# gem, not a default one), and the gem ships no `sig/`. The only `OpenStruct`
# declaration in the environment is json's add-on reopening in
# `stdlib/json/0/json.rbs` (`json_create` / `as_json` / `to_json`), so the
# class read as fully declared and `OpenStruct.new(name: "x").name` reported
# `call.undefined-method`.
#
# ostruct 0.6 defines each field as a per-instance singleton method and routes
# everything else through a private `method_missing` /
# `respond_to_missing?` pair, which is what this file declares. The
# per-field accessors cannot be declared on the class at all, so
# `Rigor::Analysis::CheckRules::CORE_OVERLAY_OPEN_RECEIVERS` also names
# `OpenStruct` as an open receiver: an RBS `method_missing` alone does not
# stop the undefined-method rule. Nothing else is declared here, so a project
# or collection signature for `OpenStruct` collides with this file only if it
# declares one of these two hooks too.
#
# Maintainer decision on #1456 (2026-10-16, option c): this narrow overlay.
# The general rule for a class declared only by another library's partial
# reopening is #1978.
class OpenStruct
  private def method_missing: (Symbol mid, *untyped args) -> untyped

  private def respond_to_missing?: (Symbol | String mid, ?bool include_private) -> bool
end
